tate  in the preceding year  um  the president of the united states  george w bush  turned the powers of american  intelligence on in ways that they hadn't  been turned on for 30 or 40 years  training them inside the borders of the  united states and also pushing the cia  to serve as kind of a paramilitary  police  abducting people  assassinating people  throwing them in  prisons  that is a misuse of the cia that is not  what the cia was created to do and in  order to do it  they've had to second uh you know  special operations commandos from the  american military  it's extraordinary  that  this president  and his relationship with the cia  who  after all have renamed their  headquarters the george bush center for  intelligence after the president's  father  um  has been so strained  in 2004 the president was asked a  question in public  so what did the cia tell you that about  what life would be like in iraq  post-saddam  and the president said  well they said it could be bad they said  it could be good they said it could be  all right they were just guessing  now just guessing is not intelligence  well now  tim you know on that question um i i  wonder if the ci doesn't deserve a  little bit more  credit as i read this  story they they did warn with some  specificity before the invasion about  the danger  of ethnic conflict and through  2003 and 2004 when the white house was  insisting everything was going great  they uh chief cessation sent  increasingly sharp  warnings and the president said what is  this guy some kind of a defeatist  exactly they were they were they were  prepared to to to reject the the  warnings so  aren't we being a little harsh and  saying these were just this was the gang  that couldn't shoot straight and didn't  know anything  donald rumsfeld secretary of defense for  the first six years  of this administration and dick cheney  vice president  had been dealing with cia  for quite a number of years going back  to the nixon and ford administrations  they were to put a politely skeptics  about the cia's ability to know the  world  during the first gulf war in 1991  the ci discovered that sanam did indeed  have an underground program to build  nuclear  weapons but they discovered it long  after the war was over and dick cheney  who was secretary of defense at that  point said  you know we blew up everything  that we knew about in iraq during the  gulf war and we didn't know about this i  will never trust the cia again and he  never has  and that has been hugely influential on  the president  whose father after all  was one of the greatest supporters the  cia has ever had  it is not  an american or unpatriotic to say we  have to do better look  the cia's i.t it's information  technology is terrible and that's by the  ci's own internal reports  fifty percent  of the people of the workforce at cia  today  are new they have less than five years  experience  and by the cia's own standard they're  not good to go overseas they're not  ready they're trainees  we as a nation have to do better at this  so let's in concluding  tim let's talk a little bit about what  the intelligence service that america  needs  might look like  and you know  based on your  reading of this of this history that's  told in legacy of ashes what do you know  that other people ought to about how  that  intelligence service might be  reconstituted  the only superpower in the world doesn't  have enough interest  in what's going on in the world to run  an espionage service and kind of threw  up his hands in despair  that was 10 years ago and things haven't  improved very much  if we want to get good at this and i  think the national survival of the  united states might depend on it because  we are in an intelligence war that may  last as long as the cold war  then we need to begin  a program  as urgent and as important  as putting a man on the moon was in the  60s  and teach high school students and  college students how to speak arabic and  chinese  and farsi  and korean  so that we can begin to know our enemy  with that hope for the future uh i want  to thank our author tim weiner author of  legacy of ashes an extraordinary look at  cia and for his thoughts about where we  where we go how we rebuild thank you  [Music]
 

